{
  "content": "**Updated conversation-analysis cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Build the task spec cumulatively from user statements only\nTreat each new user turn as a constraint update, not a fresh task. Maintain a running spec ledger of all user-stated requirements, and update it with later refinements. Earlier assistant-generated interpretations, option lists, and speculative branches do not count as settled requirements unless the user confirms them. If a later user turn narrows or clarifies the request, that newer constraint overrides older inferred interpretations.\n\n### 2) Re-evaluate against the latest user constraint, not the earlier conversation state\nThe analyzer should always compare the assistant\u2019s latest action to the most recent user message plus any still-valid prior user constraints. Do not freeze the task after an intermediate interpretation. If the user has refined the request, re-check whether prior assumptions still hold and whether the final response matches the updated target.\n\n### 3) Distinguish explicit requirements from provisional assumptions\nOnly user-stated output fields, filters, populations, ranking criteria, or deliverable formats belong in the confirmed spec. If something is merely plausible based on the conversation, keep it provisional rather than silently treating it as required. This is especially important when the user has not resolved tie-breaking, exact granularity, or whether auxiliary attributes are needed.\n\n### 4) Watch for scope narrowing and flag scope expansion\nPhrases like \u201cfocus on X\u201d usually narrow the output scope, not expand it into a menu of possibilities. If the assistant responds by enumerating multiple sub-asks, alternative outputs, or analysis branches that the user did not choose, treat that as scope expansion. The analyzer should prefer the narrowest faithful task spec and flag extra fields, joins, variants, or explanatory digressions that are no longer necessary.\n\n### 5) Use the latest user message to resolve ambiguity, but do not over-commit\nWhen the conversation is still evolving, avoid locking into one interpretation of a partially specified query. Instead, track what the user has actually fixed and what remains open. If a later user turn makes the target effectively determined, stop privileging clarification-seeking branches; repeated questioning in that state is an anchoring risk, not good caution.\n\n### 6) Track output shape separately from conceptual intent\nA response can be conceptually aligned while still failing on deliverable shape. Check whether the assistant produced the exact required artifact type and granularity: entity only, entity plus attributes, ranked row, count only, or a single final answer versus a bundle of options. Mismatches in answer shape are often the real divergence, even when the underlying logic seems close.\n\n### 7) Treat incremental edits as deltas to the full required output\nWhen the user adds a field or constraint, verify the response against the full accumulated set, not just the newest addition. Repeated mention of an already-satisfied field is usually a no-op; do not over-interpret it as a new requirement unless it changes the output. The analyzer should distinguish \u201cpartially correct\u201d from \u201ccomplete,\u201d and name the exact missing or extra element.\n\n### 8) Compare the assistant\u2019s final answer to the final spec, not to earlier assistant reasoning\nThe assistant may \u201cfix\u201d one issue while introducing another. The analyzer should focus on whether the final answer satisfies the latest user constraints after all refinements, not whether it matches an earlier assistant-generated interpretation. When compacting context, keep the stable core intent that survives refinements and drop obsolete branches so earlier false leads do not anchor later judgments.\n\n### 9) Flag over-generation and unnecessary structure\nIf the user asks for one direct result, then extra variants, parameterized alternatives, grouped summaries, or explanatory prefaces are mismatches. Multiple related outputs can be individually reasonable but still diverge from a narrowly specified ask. Prefer direct execution over analysis framing once the task is settled.\n\n### 10) Pay attention to semantic refinements in constraints\nSmall wording changes can materially alter the task. The analyzer should detect when a refinement changes the population being considered, the inclusion/exclusion rule, or the ranking criterion rather than treating it as a minor paraphrase. In particular, do not collapse \u201call items\u201d into \u201citems that satisfy an additional property\u201d when the user\u2019s latest wording changes that boundary.\n\n### 11) Separate conceptual alignment from implementation alignment\nA solution can match the intended logic but still be wrong in concrete details such as schema, grouping, join direction, or other operational choices. These implementation mismatches matter when they change the actual result set. However, if a lower-level issue is secondary and the user did not depend on exact mechanics, avoid overstating it as the primary divergence.\n\n### 12) Be alert to null-result and inclusion/exclusion semantics\nWhen the user emphasizes that results must have truly occurred, truly existed, or otherwise be non-empty in reality, suspect mistakes that would allow empty matches or irrelevant placeholders. The analyzer should treat inclusion semantics carefully when the request implies actual occurrence rather than potential association.\n\n### 13) Keep the issue report concrete and requirement-based\nPrefer naming the exact user-stated requirement that remains unsatisfied over broad quality critiques. The most useful divergence reports say what changed, what is missing, or what extra content appeared relative to the user\u2019s spec. Avoid vague \u201cbe more careful\u201d feedback unless no concrete mismatch can be identified.\n\n### 14) In multi-turn refinement, re-run the check from scratch each time\nEven if the assistant was correct in prior turns, the latest user update can invalidate the prior fit. The analyzer should not carry forward a presumption of correctness across refinements. Re-evaluate the final response against the updated ledger each time, using the current turn as the source of truth for what now matters.",
